    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d291">

                <pb facs="0680" n="638" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg638"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 291</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">665.001/8–251: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Yugoslavia (Allen) to the Secretary of
                        State </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d291-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Belgrade</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-08-02T19:00:00+01:00">August 2, 1951—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>144. In course call FonOff other subject Beam received from Assistant FonMin
                    Mates long explanation Yugo attitude re Italian peace treaty revision. Mates
                    much worried present state relations with Italy. Referred to annual spring
                    effervescence Italian Irredentis, saying in spring 1950 Yugo Govt had discussed
                    this amicably with Italian Govt but this year Italian campaign seemed to be out
                    of hand. Mates cld see little difference between De Gasperi attitude <pb
                        facs="0681" n="639" xml:id="pg639"/> and that other members Italian Govt,
                    since De Gasperi responsible for Cabinet policy. Mates believed it possible that
                    Italy in requesting revision peace treaty was more interested in obtaining
                    territorial concessions for its benefits than in amendment military provisions.
                    He said he had knowledge Italian FonOff had sent circular its missions foreign
                    countries emphasizing this aspect. Mates declared Yugo Govt certainly did not
                    regard treaty unchangeable but was afraid of opportunity it wld offer Russians
                    to spread confusion and as regards military matters to justify build-up in
                    satellites and increase pressure on Yugo. Yugo Govt believed correct procedure
                    would be endeavor obtain bilateral settlement between Yugo and Italy on
                    territorial aspects, to wit Trieste and on reparations provisions. Yugo willing
                    continue discussion Trieste in reasonable and calm spirit but objected to
                    campaigns and pressure to which it wld never yield. Discussions had already
                    taken place on reparations matters but Italy seemed to be purposely offering
                    obstruction and delay. According to Mates Yugo Govt’s idea was that attempt shld
                    be made by bilateral negotiations to reach agreement on territorial and
                    reparations issues. Thereafter Yugo Govt might inform NATO countries that it had
                    no objection to revision military provisions of peace treaty but it wld ask
                    these countries to take into account effect on Yugo position particularly
                    vis-à-vis Cominform and Russia which any increase in Italian military
                    establishment wld produce.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Allen</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
